diff --git a/docs/BlackLitterman.rst b/docs/BlackLitterman.rst
index 8a6b703..2f736c9 100644
--- a/docs/BlackLitterman.rst
+++ b/docs/BlackLitterman.rst
@@ -218,6 +218,22 @@ inherits from ``BaseOptimizer``, this follows the same API as the ``EfficientFro
     bl.bl_weights(delta)
     weights = bl.clean_weights()
 
+.. caution::
+
+    Like every optimizer in this library, ``bl_weights()`` rescales its solution to sum to one. That rescaling
+    cancels :math:`\delta` exactly, because the raw weights above are proportional to :math:`1/\delta`. Passing a
+    ``delta`` to ``bl_weights()`` therefore does not change the weights it returns - the argument is accepted so the
+    signature matches the rest of the library, and the line above is included only to mirror the formula. Risk
+    aversion does bite in the two places that consume it before any normalisation: the ``risk_aversion`` argument of
+    ``BlackLittermanModel``, which scales the market-implied prior, and ``EfficientFrontier.max_quadratic_utility``.
+
+    That same rescaling has no answer when the implied portfolio is net short, which is what broadly negative
+    posterior returns produce: dividing by a negative net would invert every position, and dividing by a net near
+    zero would blow the weights up. ``bl_weights()`` raises a ``ValueError`` in that case rather than return either.
+    If you hit it, use the posterior estimates the way the literature suggests - feed ``bl_returns()`` and
+    ``bl_cov()`` into an optimizer, which can express a net-short or market-neutral book that a fully-invested
+    weight vector cannot.
+
 
